      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 IJS Publishing Group Limited. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "Transaxillary robotic thyroid surgery was pioneered in South Korea where cultural factors, anthropometry and remuneration favour this. Small thyroid nodules account for the majority of cases due to a national thyroid cancer screening programme. However, the technique has not been evaluated in the United Kingdom where larger thyroid nodules tend to undergo surgery in a patient population with a higher body mass index (BMI)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Long term prospective non-randomised study. Sixteen consecutive robotic hemithyroidectomy patients were compared to 16 open controls."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "There were no robotic conversions to open and no significant difference regarding pain, voice, or quality of life (QoL). In the robotic group, long term. scar cosmesis at 3 years was superior (p = 0.02) although the operative time was significantly longer (228 min vs. 85 min, p = 0.01). One patient had a transient recurrent laryngeal nerve paresis and another had temporary shoulder dysfunction. Both resolved in 4 weeks."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "DISCUSSION",
            "content": "This study highlights the considerable difference between a Western patient population compared to South East Asia. Despite this robotic thyroidectomy is feasible and safe in a UK population."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Despite a low uptake in the UK compared to the Far East, robotic thyroidectomy represents a viable option for selected patients, achieving superior cosmesis compared with conventional thyroidectomy at the expense of time and cost. The key is careful patient selection. A randomised study is needed to establish the clinical efficacy compared to conventional surgery in this population."
          }
        ]
      },
